1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,700
Your mother's kitchen in the nineteen sixties

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:03,541
was her pride.

3
00:00:03,721 --> 00:00:08,225
Gleaming appliances in harvest gold or turquoise.

4
00:00:08,405 --> 00:00:09,833
A spice rack.

5
00:00:10,013 --> 00:00:12,773
A row of canisters for flour,

6
00:00:12,773 --> 00:00:14,866
sugar, coffee and tea.

7
00:00:15,046 --> 00:00:17,532
A cabinet full of new products from

8
00:00:17,532 --> 00:00:20,585
the supermarket, each one promising to make

9
00:00:20,585 --> 00:00:21,722
her life easier,

10
00:00:21,722 --> 00:00:24,775
her family healthier and her house cleaner.

11
00:00:24,955 --> 00:00:26,767
And she trusted every one of them.

12
00:00:27,117 --> 00:00:28,243
Why wouldn't she?

13
00:00:28,423 --> 00:00:30,390
The products came from big,

14
00:00:30,390 --> 00:00:31,848
respected companies.

15
00:00:32,028 --> 00:00:35,405
They were advertised on television by friendly

16
00:00:35,405 --> 00:00:37,020
actors in white coats.

17
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:38,790
Doctors endorsed them.

18
00:00:38,970 --> 00:00:40,921
Magazines praised them.

19
00:00:41,101 --> 00:00:42,199
In the sixties,

20
00:00:42,199 --> 00:00:45,127
Americans had enormous faith in science,

21
00:00:45,127 --> 00:00:47,103
progress and modern living.

22
00:00:47,454 --> 00:00:49,438
But some of those things turned out

23
00:00:49,438 --> 00:00:51,422
to be very different from what they

24
00:00:51,422 --> 00:00:51,819
seemed.

25
00:00:51,999 --> 00:00:53,813
And some of the things she was

26
00:00:53,813 --> 00:00:56,232
told were old-fashioned turned out to be

27
00:00:56,232 --> 00:00:57,200
right all along.

28
00:00:57,380 --> 00:00:59,832
So let's open the cupboards of the

29
00:00:59,832 --> 00:01:01,634
nineteen sixties kitchen,

30
00:01:01,634 --> 00:01:02,427
one by one,

31
00:01:02,427 --> 00:01:04,230
and see what we know now.

32
00:01:04,579 --> 00:01:05,961
Let's start under the sink.

33
00:01:06,141 --> 00:01:07,915
In many sixties kitchens,

34
00:01:07,915 --> 00:01:09,972
there was a can of bug spray,

35
00:01:09,972 --> 00:01:11,888
and it often contained DDT.

36
00:01:12,068 --> 00:01:15,186
During the fifties and early sixties,

37
00:01:15,186 --> 00:01:17,629
DDT was considered a miracle.

38
00:01:17,809 --> 00:01:19,553
It killed mosquitoes,

39
00:01:19,553 --> 00:01:21,048
flies and roaches.

40
00:01:21,228 --> 00:01:22,835
It was sprayed on crops,

41
00:01:22,835 --> 00:01:25,380
in homes, and even from trucks driving

42
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:28,125
through neighborhoods on summer evenings.

43
00:01:28,475 --> 00:01:30,742
Kids ran behind the fogging trucks,

44
00:01:30,742 --> 00:01:32,492
playing in the white cloud.

45
00:01:32,672 --> 00:01:34,657
Nobody thought it was dangerous.

46
00:01:34,837 --> 00:01:37,156
Then, in nineteen sixty-two,

47
00:01:37,156 --> 00:01:40,718
a biologist named Rachel Carson published a

48
00:01:40,718 --> 00:01:42,871
book called Silent Spring.

49
00:01:43,051 --> 00:01:45,895
She showed that DDT was killing birds

50
00:01:45,895 --> 00:01:48,508
and building up in the food chain.

51
00:01:48,688 --> 00:01:50,673
The book caused a sensation.

52
00:01:50,853 --> 00:01:52,891
In nineteen seventy-two,

53
00:01:52,891 --> 00:01:55,864
DDT was banned in the United States

54
00:01:55,864 --> 00:01:57,053
for most uses.

55
00:01:57,402 --> 00:01:59,597
Also under the sink were the cleaning

56
00:01:59,597 --> 00:02:00,131
products.

57
00:02:00,311 --> 00:02:03,239
Bleach, ammonia, scouring powder,

58
00:02:03,239 --> 00:02:06,255
oven cleaner and furniture polish.

59
00:02:06,435 --> 00:02:08,004
Mothers used them all,

60
00:02:08,004 --> 00:02:10,358
often in a small kitchen with the

61
00:02:10,358 --> 00:02:11,427
windows closed.

62
00:02:11,607 --> 00:02:14,246
Some even mixed bleach and ammonia,

63
00:02:14,246 --> 00:02:17,564
thinking two cleaners would work better than

64
00:02:17,564 --> 00:02:17,865
one.

65
00:02:18,045 --> 00:02:20,452
That mixture releases a toxic gas,

66
00:02:20,452 --> 00:02:22,575
and it sent many people to the

67
00:02:22,575 --> 00:02:23,212
hospital.

68
00:02:23,561 --> 00:02:26,313
And the laundry detergent of the sixties

69
00:02:26,313 --> 00:02:28,171
was loaded with phosphates.

70
00:02:28,351 --> 00:02:30,074
They made clothes whiter,

71
00:02:30,074 --> 00:02:32,280
but when the water went down the

72
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:34,762
drain, they fed huge blooms of algae

73
00:02:34,762 --> 00:02:36,141
in lakes and rivers.

74
00:02:36,321 --> 00:02:39,667
Lake Erie became so polluted that newspapers

75
00:02:39,667 --> 00:02:40,884
called it dying.

76
00:02:41,064 --> 00:02:42,764
In the early seventies,

77
00:02:42,764 --> 00:02:45,944
many states banned or limited phosphates in

78
00:02:45,944 --> 00:02:49,272
laundry soap, and the lakes slowly recovered.

79
00:02:49,621 --> 00:02:50,882
On the kitchen table,

80
00:02:50,882 --> 00:02:53,103
there might have been a little bottle

81
00:02:53,103 --> 00:02:54,544
of artificial sweetener.

82
00:02:54,724 --> 00:02:55,784
In the sixties,

83
00:02:55,784 --> 00:02:57,975
diet sodas became very popular,

84
00:02:57,975 --> 00:03:00,520
and many of them were sweetened with

85
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:02,499
a chemical called cyclamate,

86
00:03:02,499 --> 00:03:04,407
often mixed with saccharin.

87
00:03:04,587 --> 00:03:07,304
Tab, Diet Rite and Fresca.

88
00:03:07,484 --> 00:03:09,835
Women trying to stay slim drank them

89
00:03:09,835 --> 00:03:10,618
by the case.

90
00:03:10,968 --> 00:03:12,812
In nineteen sixty-nine,

91
00:03:12,812 --> 00:03:15,536
a study found that very high doses

92
00:03:15,536 --> 00:03:18,341
of cyclamate caused cancer in rats.

93
00:03:18,521 --> 00:03:20,947
The government banned it almost overnight.

94
00:03:21,127 --> 00:03:24,238
Companies had to reformulate their diet drinks

95
00:03:24,238 --> 00:03:24,982
in a hurry.

96
00:03:25,162 --> 00:03:28,801
Later research questioned whether cyclamate was really

97
00:03:28,801 --> 00:03:30,351
as dangerous as feared,

98
00:03:30,351 --> 00:03:32,777
and it's still allowed in many other

99
00:03:32,777 --> 00:03:33,451
countries.

100
00:03:33,631 --> 00:03:34,722
But in America,

101
00:03:34,722 --> 00:03:36,104
it never came back.

102
00:03:36,454 --> 00:03:37,631
In the cupboard,

103
00:03:37,631 --> 00:03:39,396
there was food coloring,

104
00:03:39,396 --> 00:03:42,263
and many packaged foods contained a dye

105
00:03:42,263 --> 00:03:44,175
called Red Dye Number Two.

106
00:03:44,355 --> 00:03:45,891
It was in candy,

107
00:03:45,891 --> 00:03:47,908
cake mixes, sausages,

108
00:03:47,908 --> 00:03:50,404
ice cream and soft drinks.

109
00:03:50,584 --> 00:03:53,208
It made maraschino cherries bright red.

110
00:03:53,388 --> 00:03:55,158
It was one of the most common

111
00:03:55,158 --> 00:03:56,685
food dyes in the country.

112
00:03:57,034 --> 00:03:58,257
In the seventies,

113
00:03:58,257 --> 00:04:01,348
studies raised concerns that Red Dye Number

114
00:04:01,348 --> 00:04:03,002
Two might cause cancer.

115
00:04:03,182 --> 00:04:04,945
In nineteen seventy-six,

116
00:04:04,945 --> 00:04:06,781
the government banned it.

117
00:04:06,961 --> 00:04:09,103
The scare was so big that the

118
00:04:09,103 --> 00:04:11,392
Mars company took red M and M's

119
00:04:11,392 --> 00:04:13,533
out of the bag for almost ten

120
00:04:13,533 --> 00:04:16,339
years, even though they had never used

121
00:04:16,339 --> 00:04:17,004
that dye.

122
00:04:17,184 --> 00:04:19,316
Red M and M's didn't come back

123
00:04:19,316 --> 00:04:21,305
until nineteen eighty-seven.

124
00:04:21,655 --> 00:04:24,036
Even the kitchen walls held a secret.

125
00:04:24,216 --> 00:04:27,204
Many homes built before nineteen fifty,

126
00:04:27,204 --> 00:04:28,813
and some built later,

127
00:04:28,813 --> 00:04:31,495
were painted with lead-based paint.

128
00:04:31,675 --> 00:04:32,629
It was durable,

129
00:04:32,629 --> 00:04:34,027
and it came in bright,

130
00:04:34,027 --> 00:04:36,507
washable colors that were perfect for a

131
00:04:36,507 --> 00:04:37,016
kitchen.

132
00:04:37,196 --> 00:04:38,978
When it chipped or peeled,

133
00:04:38,978 --> 00:04:41,858
small children sometimes put the flakes in

134
00:04:41,858 --> 00:04:45,218
their mouths, because lead tastes slightly sweet.

135
00:04:45,568 --> 00:04:48,756
Doctors slowly learned that even small amounts

136
00:04:48,756 --> 00:04:51,112
of lead could harm a child's brain

137
00:04:51,112 --> 00:04:52,221
and development.

138
00:04:52,401 --> 00:04:54,406
In nineteen seventy-eight,

139
00:04:54,406 --> 00:04:56,873
lead paint was banned for use in

140
00:04:56,873 --> 00:04:57,335
homes.

141
00:04:57,515 --> 00:05:00,418
Leaded gasoline was phased out over the

142
00:05:00,418 --> 00:05:01,683
next two decades.

143
00:05:01,863 --> 00:05:04,134
Since then, the amount of lead in

144
00:05:04,134 --> 00:05:07,575
American children's blood has fallen dramatically.

145
00:05:07,925 --> 00:05:09,324
In the refrigerator,

146
00:05:09,324 --> 00:05:10,723
there was margarine.

147
00:05:10,903 --> 00:05:11,917
In the sixties,

148
00:05:11,917 --> 00:05:15,297
doctors and advertisers told Americans that butter

149
00:05:15,297 --> 00:05:16,785
was bad for the heart,

150
00:05:16,785 --> 00:05:19,286
and margarine was the healthy choice.

151
00:05:19,466 --> 00:05:21,869
Families switched by the millions.

152
00:05:22,049 --> 00:05:25,788
Crisco shortening replaced lard in pie crusts.

153
00:05:25,968 --> 00:05:27,709
Margarine went on toast,

154
00:05:27,709 --> 00:05:29,959
in cookies, and on every ear of

155
00:05:29,959 --> 00:05:30,321
corn.

156
00:05:30,671 --> 00:05:33,632
But much of that margarine and shortening

157
00:05:33,632 --> 00:05:36,666
was made with partially hydrogenated oils,

158
00:05:36,666 --> 00:05:38,832
full of what we now call trans

159
00:05:38,832 --> 00:05:39,193
fats.

160
00:05:39,373 --> 00:05:42,404
Decades later, scientists found that trans fats

161
00:05:42,404 --> 00:05:44,854
were actually worse for the heart than

162
00:05:44,854 --> 00:05:45,306
butter.

163
00:05:45,486 --> 00:05:46,712
In twenty fifteen,

164
00:05:46,712 --> 00:05:49,708
the government ordered them removed from the

165
00:05:49,708 --> 00:05:50,525
food supply.

166
00:05:50,705 --> 00:05:52,868
Grandma's butter, it turned out,

167
00:05:52,868 --> 00:05:54,153
wasn't the villain.

168
00:05:54,502 --> 00:05:56,523
On the stove were the new pans.

169
00:05:56,703 --> 00:05:57,815
In the sixties,

170
00:05:57,815 --> 00:06:01,080
Teflon nonstick cookware became a sensation.

171
00:06:01,260 --> 00:06:02,955
Eggs slid right off.

172
00:06:03,135 --> 00:06:04,214
No scrubbing.

173
00:06:04,394 --> 00:06:06,397
Teflon was made by DuPont,

174
00:06:06,397 --> 00:06:08,785
and it seemed like a miracle of

175
00:06:08,785 --> 00:06:10,095
modern chemistry.

176
00:06:10,275 --> 00:06:13,043
Many women put away their heavy old

177
00:06:13,043 --> 00:06:15,970
cast iron skillets and bought a shiny

178
00:06:15,970 --> 00:06:16,602
new set.

179
00:06:16,953 --> 00:06:20,505
Years later, scientists raised concerns about a

180
00:06:20,505 --> 00:06:22,092
chemical called PFOA,

181
00:06:22,092 --> 00:06:24,511
which was used in making Teflon.

182
00:06:24,691 --> 00:06:27,153
It was found in water supplies near

183
00:06:27,153 --> 00:06:29,404
factories and in people's blood.

184
00:06:29,584 --> 00:06:30,874
By twenty fifteen,

185
00:06:30,874 --> 00:06:33,741
American companies had stopped using it.

186
00:06:33,921 --> 00:06:36,161
And many cooks went right back to

187
00:06:36,161 --> 00:06:39,148
the cast iron skillet their grandmothers had

188
00:06:39,148 --> 00:06:40,167
used all along.

189
00:06:40,517 --> 00:06:42,463
And here's a scare that went the

190
00:06:42,463 --> 00:06:43,071
other way.

191
00:06:43,251 --> 00:06:44,532
In the sixties,

192
00:06:44,532 --> 00:06:47,693
many kitchens had a shaker of Accent,

193
00:06:47,693 --> 00:06:51,708
a flavor enhancer made of monosodium glutamate,

194
00:06:51,708 --> 00:06:52,306
or MSG.

195
00:06:52,486 --> 00:06:54,661
Mothers sprinkled it on meat,

196
00:06:54,661 --> 00:06:56,237
soups and vegetables.

197
00:06:56,417 --> 00:06:58,534
Then, in nineteen sixty-eight,

198
00:06:58,534 --> 00:07:00,511
a doctor wrote a letter to a

199
00:07:00,511 --> 00:07:03,970
medical journal describing symptoms he felt after

200
00:07:03,970 --> 00:07:06,088
eating at Chinese restaurants.

201
00:07:06,438 --> 00:07:08,404
The idea spread quickly,

202
00:07:08,404 --> 00:07:12,010
and for decades many Americans believed that

203
00:07:12,010 --> 00:07:13,485
MSG was dangerous.

204
00:07:13,665 --> 00:07:16,451
Restaurants put up signs saying No MSG.

205
00:07:16,631 --> 00:07:19,402
But careful studies over many years have

206
00:07:19,402 --> 00:07:21,827
mostly failed to find any real harm

207
00:07:21,827 --> 00:07:22,935
for most people,

208
00:07:22,935 --> 00:07:25,014
and the FDA considers it safe.

209
00:07:25,194 --> 00:07:28,091
Glutamate occurs naturally in tomatoes,

210
00:07:28,091 --> 00:07:29,652
cheese and mushrooms.

211
00:07:29,832 --> 00:07:32,879
Your mother's shaker of Accent was probably

212
00:07:32,879 --> 00:07:33,942
fine all along.

213
00:07:34,292 --> 00:07:35,220
In the cabinet,

214
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:36,892
there were the good dishes.

215
00:07:37,072 --> 00:07:39,092
Many families had Fiesta ware,

216
00:07:39,092 --> 00:07:41,986
the brightly colored dinnerware made by the

217
00:07:41,986 --> 00:07:45,083
Homer Laughlin China Company in West Virginia.

218
00:07:45,263 --> 00:07:47,619
Red, yellow, turquoise,

219
00:07:47,619 --> 00:07:49,872
green and cobalt blue.

220
00:07:50,052 --> 00:07:51,654
It's still collected today.

221
00:07:51,834 --> 00:07:55,485
But the original red-orange glaze contained uranium

222
00:07:55,485 --> 00:07:58,348
oxide, which made it mildly radioactive.

223
00:07:58,698 --> 00:08:01,623
For decades, families ate off those plates

224
00:08:01,623 --> 00:08:02,738
without knowing.

225
00:08:02,918 --> 00:08:05,681
Some Geiger counters still click near them

226
00:08:05,681 --> 00:08:06,076
today.

227
00:08:06,256 --> 00:08:09,331
Experts say the risk from occasional use

228
00:08:09,331 --> 00:08:12,406
is small, but collectors are advised not

229
00:08:12,406 --> 00:08:14,789
to eat from the old red pieces.

230
00:08:14,969 --> 00:08:17,794
Some older china and pottery also used

231
00:08:17,794 --> 00:08:19,355
lead in their glazes,

232
00:08:19,355 --> 00:08:22,329
which could leach into acidic foods like

233
00:08:22,329 --> 00:08:24,559
tomato sauce and orange juice.

234
00:08:24,909 --> 00:08:27,521
Even the refrigerator had a hidden danger.

235
00:08:27,701 --> 00:08:29,323
In the forties and fifties,

236
00:08:29,323 --> 00:08:31,966
refrigerators closed with a heavy latch that

237
00:08:31,966 --> 00:08:34,249
could only be opened from the outside.

238
00:08:34,429 --> 00:08:37,155
When old refrigerators were thrown out in

239
00:08:37,155 --> 00:08:39,017
alleys, dumps and backyards,

240
00:08:39,017 --> 00:08:42,076
children climbed inside to play hide-and-seek,

241
00:08:42,076 --> 00:08:43,473
and couldn't get out.

242
00:08:43,653 --> 00:08:46,822
Tragically, many children died this way.

243
00:08:47,173 --> 00:08:48,740
In nineteen fifty-six,

244
00:08:48,740 --> 00:08:51,875
Congress passed the Refrigerator Safety Act.

245
00:08:52,055 --> 00:08:54,604
It required that new refrigerators could be

246
00:08:54,604 --> 00:08:55,967
opened from the inside.

247
00:08:56,147 --> 00:08:59,045
That's why refrigerator doors today close with

248
00:08:59,045 --> 00:08:59,549
magnets.

249
00:08:59,729 --> 00:09:01,441
And for years afterward,

250
00:09:01,441 --> 00:09:04,509
parents and teachers warned children never,

251
00:09:04,509 --> 00:09:07,078
ever to play in an old refrigerator.

252
00:09:07,428 --> 00:09:09,239
And in many sixties kitchens,

253
00:09:09,239 --> 00:09:11,363
there was an ashtray on the table.

254
00:09:11,543 --> 00:09:14,132
Roughly four in ten adults smoked.

255
00:09:14,312 --> 00:09:16,456
Mothers smoked while cooking,

256
00:09:16,456 --> 00:09:18,526
fathers smoked after dinner,

257
00:09:18,526 --> 00:09:20,744
and guests smoked over coffee.

258
00:09:20,924 --> 00:09:23,478
Children grew up breathing it every day.

259
00:09:23,658 --> 00:09:26,191
Pregnant women were often told that a

260
00:09:26,191 --> 00:09:27,902
few cigarettes were fine,

261
00:09:27,902 --> 00:09:28,860
even relaxing.

262
00:09:29,210 --> 00:09:30,868
In nineteen sixty-four,

263
00:09:30,868 --> 00:09:34,184
the Surgeon General's report linked smoking to

264
00:09:34,184 --> 00:09:35,049
lung cancer.

265
00:09:35,229 --> 00:09:38,994
Warning labels appeared on packs in nineteen

266
00:09:38,994 --> 00:09:39,850
sixty-six.

267
00:09:40,030 --> 00:09:43,119
Cigarette ads were banned from television in

268
00:09:43,119 --> 00:09:44,593
nineteen seventy-one.

269
00:09:44,773 --> 00:09:46,308
Over the next decades,

270
00:09:46,308 --> 00:09:48,053
millions of parents quit,

271
00:09:48,053 --> 00:09:51,123
often because their children begged them to.

272
00:09:51,474 --> 00:09:53,536
But here's the other side of the

273
00:09:53,536 --> 00:09:53,923
story.

274
00:09:54,103 --> 00:09:55,764
A lot of what your mother did

275
00:09:55,764 --> 00:09:57,597
in that kitchen turned out to be

276
00:09:57,597 --> 00:09:58,399
exactly right.

277
00:09:58,579 --> 00:10:00,413
She cooked from scratch.

278
00:10:00,593 --> 00:10:03,259
She used fresh vegetables from the garden

279
00:10:03,259 --> 00:10:04,169
or the market.

280
00:10:04,349 --> 00:10:06,614
She made soup from leftovers,

281
00:10:06,614 --> 00:10:08,645
and wasted almost nothing.

282
00:10:08,825 --> 00:10:11,628
Today, nutrition experts tell us to do

283
00:10:11,628 --> 00:10:12,586
exactly that.

284
00:10:12,937 --> 00:10:15,096
She served smaller portions.

285
00:10:15,276 --> 00:10:18,199
A sixties dinner plate was about nine

286
00:10:18,199 --> 00:10:19,699
or ten inches wide.

287
00:10:19,879 --> 00:10:22,835
Today, many dinner plates are eleven or

288
00:10:22,835 --> 00:10:23,896
twelve inches.

289
00:10:24,076 --> 00:10:26,214
A soft drink came in a little

290
00:10:26,214 --> 00:10:28,720
six-and-a-half-ounce glass bottle.

291
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:31,037
A candy bar was smaller.

292
00:10:31,217 --> 00:10:33,602
Families ate snacks less often,

293
00:10:33,602 --> 00:10:35,448
and dessert was a treat,

294
00:10:35,448 --> 00:10:36,371
not a habit.

295
00:10:36,721 --> 00:10:39,252
She also canned and preserved.

296
00:10:39,432 --> 00:10:41,395
Tomatoes, green beans,

297
00:10:41,395 --> 00:10:42,912
jams and pickles,

298
00:10:42,912 --> 00:10:45,946
put up in glass jars every summer.

299
00:10:46,126 --> 00:10:47,527
It was a lot of work,

300
00:10:47,527 --> 00:10:49,661
but she knew exactly what was in

301
00:10:49,661 --> 00:10:50,328
every jar.

302
00:10:50,508 --> 00:10:53,079
Today, home canning has come back into

303
00:10:53,079 --> 00:10:56,462
fashion, and many young families are rediscovering

304
00:10:56,462 --> 00:10:59,371
the skills that their grandmothers took for

305
00:10:59,371 --> 00:10:59,913
granted.

306
00:11:00,262 --> 00:11:02,898
She used cast iron and glass.

307
00:11:03,078 --> 00:11:05,641
Pyrex baking dishes and Corning Ware,

308
00:11:05,641 --> 00:11:07,996
which went from the freezer to the

309
00:11:07,996 --> 00:11:09,243
oven to the table.

310
00:11:09,423 --> 00:11:11,819
Those dishes last for generations,

311
00:11:11,819 --> 00:11:14,779
and many families still use their mothers'

312
00:11:14,779 --> 00:11:16,400
casserole dishes today.

313
00:11:16,580 --> 00:11:18,150
Some of those patterns,

314
00:11:18,150 --> 00:11:20,607
like Spring Blossom and Butterprint,

315
00:11:20,607 --> 00:11:22,722
are now treasured collectibles.

316
00:11:23,073 --> 00:11:25,127
And she didn't waste anything.

317
00:11:25,307 --> 00:11:27,258
Bacon grease was saved in a can

318
00:11:27,258 --> 00:11:28,767
by the stove for frying.

319
00:11:28,947 --> 00:11:32,314
Stale bread became bread pudding or stuffing.

320
00:11:32,494 --> 00:11:34,514
Chicken bones became broth.

321
00:11:34,694 --> 00:11:37,028
Old clothes became dish rags.

322
00:11:37,208 --> 00:11:39,460
The idea that you should reduce,

323
00:11:39,460 --> 00:11:42,627
reuse and recycle was something she practiced

324
00:11:42,627 --> 00:11:45,160
every day, long before anyone made a

325
00:11:45,160 --> 00:11:46,357
slogan out of it.

326
00:11:46,707 --> 00:11:48,939
And she sat the whole family down

327
00:11:48,939 --> 00:11:49,819
at the table,

328
00:11:49,819 --> 00:11:50,631
every night.

329
00:11:50,811 --> 00:11:53,845
Researchers today have found that children who

330
00:11:53,845 --> 00:11:56,286
eat regular family dinners tend to do

331
00:11:56,286 --> 00:11:57,408
better in school,

332
00:11:57,408 --> 00:12:00,112
eat healthier, and have fewer problems as

333
00:12:00,112 --> 00:12:00,772
teenagers.

334
00:12:00,952 --> 00:12:02,901
Your mother didn't need a study to

335
00:12:02,901 --> 00:12:03,704
tell her that.

336
00:12:03,884 --> 00:12:04,812
She just knew.

337
00:12:05,163 --> 00:12:07,421
So what's the lesson of the sixties

338
00:12:07,421 --> 00:12:07,937
kitchen?

339
00:12:08,117 --> 00:12:10,393
It's not that our mothers were foolish.

340
00:12:10,573 --> 00:12:12,371
They trusted the experts,

341
00:12:12,371 --> 00:12:15,680
the scientists, the doctors and the companies,

342
00:12:15,680 --> 00:12:17,910
just like most people do today.

343
00:12:18,090 --> 00:12:20,486
Some of what the experts said turned

344
00:12:20,486 --> 00:12:21,550
out to be wrong.

345
00:12:21,730 --> 00:12:23,945
And some of what grandma did turned

346
00:12:23,945 --> 00:12:24,958
out to be right.

347
00:12:25,308 --> 00:12:26,607
Maybe in fifty years,

348
00:12:26,607 --> 00:12:28,896
people will look back at our kitchens

349
00:12:28,896 --> 00:12:30,938
and wonder what we were thinking.

350
00:12:31,118 --> 00:12:33,206
The things we trust today might be

351
00:12:33,206 --> 00:12:35,356
the stories our grandchildren tell.

352
00:12:35,536 --> 00:12:37,219
That's the way it always goes.

353
00:12:37,570 --> 00:12:39,590
But whatever was in those cupboards,

354
00:12:39,590 --> 00:12:41,498
what most of us remember about our

355
00:12:41,498 --> 00:12:43,630
mothers' kitchens isn't the chemicals.

356
00:12:43,810 --> 00:12:45,975
It's the smell of pot roast on

357
00:12:45,975 --> 00:12:46,480
Sunday.

358
00:12:46,660 --> 00:12:48,239
The sound of the mixer.

359
00:12:48,419 --> 00:12:49,673
Licking the beaters.

360
00:12:49,853 --> 00:12:51,769
And the feeling of being at home,

361
00:12:51,769 --> 00:12:53,336
with someone who loved you,

362
00:12:53,336 --> 00:12:54,149
making dinner.

363
00:12:54,499 --> 00:12:56,333
So tell us in the comments.

364
00:12:56,513 --> 00:12:58,455
What was in your mother's kitchen that

365
00:12:58,455 --> 00:12:59,938
would never be allowed today?

366
00:13:00,118 --> 00:13:01,775
And what did she do that you

367
00:13:01,775 --> 00:13:02,544
still do now?

368
00:13:02,724 --> 00:13:04,187
We read every comment.

369
00:13:04,367 --> 00:13:06,678
Next time, we're going to the suburbs

370
00:13:06,678 --> 00:13:08,176
of nineteen fifty-eight,

371
00:13:08,176 --> 00:13:10,487
and finding out why everyone left the

372
00:13:10,487 --> 00:13:10,799
city.

373
00:13:10,979 --> 00:13:12,880
Thank you for spending this time with

374
00:13:12,880 --> 00:13:13,034
me.
